  "not_simulated": [
    {
      "what": "the bias ladder's resistance",
      "pins": ["V0"],
      "why": "The controller makes its LCD levels on a ladder of five equal resistors from VDD to V0 (Hitachi Figure 21, p. 37), so an open V0 sits at VDD and the glass is blank. Neither sheet gives the ladder's value, so 10 kohm stands in for it, and a contrast pot's wiper on V0 reads a little higher than the pot alone would give.",
    },
    {
      "what": "the module's supply range",
      "pins": ["VDD"],
      "why": "Vishay rates the module 4.7 V to 5.3 V (p. 1). The controller powers up once VDD rises to 2.7 V and keeps running until VDD falls below 2.4 V (Hitachi gives no falling threshold, so 2.4 V is an estimate), and the glass needs VDD - V0 of at least 3.0 V. A bus cycle while powered with VDD below 4.7 V runs and warns chip.protocol rule low_supply. A supply between 5.3 V and 7.0 V does not warn, and over 7.0 V warns rating.voltage.",
    },
    {
      "what": "blue transmissive visibility",
      "pins": ["A", "K"],
      "why": "On a blue negative transmissive 1602 LCD, characters are only visible while the backlight is lit. `@<lcd>.text` is blank while the display is off, the backlight is dark, or VDD - V0 is under 3.0 V.",
    },
  ],
